  "result": "**More agents do not always give better results.** Neither five nor ten is the right starting point.\n\n**Why this matters:** your budget is small. Every agent you add costs more.\n\n**What happens when you split:**\n- Each agent reads its own instructions and context. You pay for those tokens again.\n- Agents pass work to each other as text. Details get lost at each hand-off.\n- More parts mean more places to fail, and more to explain at your design review.\n\n**Example:** Ten friends plan one dinner. Each friend must call the others to share the plan. The calls take longer than the cooking.\n\n**When a split pays off:**\n- The tasks are truly separate.\n- One agent's context would get too large.\n- The tasks need different tools.\n\n**What to do:** Start with one agent. Give it three tools: search manuals, check calendar, book time. Split only when you measure a real problem. Booking might be a fixed workflow, not an agent.\n\nI can teach a full lesson on this later.\n\n**Question:** Answering a manual question and booking a machine both happen in one chat. Name one cost you add if you give each its own agent.",
